= February 2026 — An alliance is not one relationship

<span class=""><b>Tom Rose</b></span>, the United States ambassador to Poland under President **Donald
Trump**, announces that he will have no further dealings with **Włodzimierz
Czarzasty**, the New Left politician now serving as Marshal of the Sejm.
Czarzasty had rejected a Polish nomination of Trump for the Nobel Peace Prize
and accused Washington of treating allies as clients.

The empty chair is personal and institutional at once. Rose can deny the
Marshal a meeting; he cannot close Congress, NATO staffs, the Polish Foreign
Ministry, the Palace or the Sejm's own committees. Czarzasty can defend the
chamber; he cannot turn his party quarrel into a national rupture without the
government and President.

Lewica must decide whether to make the boycott a question of parliamentary
dignity, give Rose a route back, force the disagreement into formal rules or
sacrifice its own Marshal's authority for White House access. Its Atlanticists
fear isolation. Its parliamentary wing fears learning that an ambassador may
choose which constitutional officer counts.

[? if gaza_chain_stage >= 3 and gaza_stance_score >= 2: The embassy's briefing
note does not begin in February. It begins in October 2023, with the ceasefire
demand, and continues through Soból's convoy and the Warsaw campus. Rose is not
answering one sentence about a Nobel nomination; he is answering three years of
them.?]
[? if gaza_chain_stage >= 3 and gaza_stance_score <= -2: Rose's staff are
genuinely surprised. This is the party that stood with Israel after 7 October,
accepted the army's own inquiry into Damian Soból's death and backed the rector
against the encampment. The boycott is aimed at a Marshal, not at a record, and
everyone in the room knows the difference.?]

- @ambassador_stand
- @ambassador_clarify
- @ambassador_committee
- @ambassador_rules
- @ambassador_apologise

